      "text": "To solve the equation \\( 7^{x+7} = 8^x \\), we start by taking the natural logarithm of both sides of the equation. This gives us:\n\n\\[\n\\ln(7^{x+7}) = \\ln(8^x)\n\\]\n\nUsing the logarithm power rule \\(\\ln(a^b) = b \\ln(a)\\), we can rewrite the equation as:\n\n\\[\n(x+7) \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8\n\\]\n\nNext, we distribute \\(\\ln 7\\) on the left side:\n\n\\[\nx \\ln 7 + 7 \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8\n\\]\n\nTo isolate \\(x\\), we first move all terms involving \\(x\\) to one side of the equation:\n\n\\[\nx \\ln 7 - x \\ln 8 = -7 \\ln 7\n\\]\n\nFactor out \\(x\\) on the left side:\n\n\\[\nx (\\ln 7 - \\ln 8) = -7 \\ln 7\n\\]\n\nNow, solve for \\(x\\) by dividing both sides by \\(\\ln 7 - \\ln 8\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8}\n\\]\n\nWe are given that the solution can be expressed in the form \\(x = \\log_b 7^7\\). Therefore, we need to express \\(\\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8}\\) in the form \\(\\log_b 7^7\\). Recall that \\(\\log_b a = \\frac{\\ln a}{\\ln b}\\), so we have:\n\n\\[\n\\log_b 7^7 = \\frac{\\ln 7^7}{\\ln b} = \\frac{7 \\ln 7}{\\ln b}\n\\]\n\nBy comparing this with \\(x = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8}\\), we can see that \\(\\ln b = \\ln 8\\), since \\(\\ln 7 - \\ln 8 = \\ln 7^7 - \\ln 7 = \\ln 7 \\left( \\frac{7 - 1}{7} \\right) = \\ln 7 \\cdot \\frac{6}{7}\\) (using the property \\(\\ln a^b = b \\ln a\\)). Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\ln b = \\ln 8 \\implies b = 8\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(b\\) is \\(\\boxed{8}\\).",
