      "text": "To solve the equation \\(7^{x+7} = 8^x\\) and express the solution in the form \\(x = \\log_b 7^7\\), we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Express the equation in a more manageable form:**\n   \\[\n   7^{x+7} = 8^x\n   \\]\n   Using the properties of exponents, we can rewrite the left side:\n   \\[\n   7^x \\cdot 7^7 = 8^x\n   \\]\n   This can be rearranged to:\n   \\[\n   7^x \\cdot 7^7 = 8^x\n   \\]\n   Dividing both sides by \\(7^x\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   7^7 = \\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)^x\n   \\]\n\n2. **Take the logarithm of both sides:**\n   To solve for \\(x\\), we take the logarithm of both sides. We can use the natural logarithm (ln) or the common logarithm (log), but we will use the natural logarithm for convenience:\n   \\[\n   \\ln(7^7) = \\ln\\left(\\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)^x\\right)\n   \\]\n   Using the property of logarithms \\(\\ln(a^b) = b \\ln(a)\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   7 \\ln(7) = x \\ln\\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n3. **Solve for \\(x\\):**\n   To isolate \\(x\\), we divide both sides by \\(\\ln\\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)\\):\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{7 \\ln(7)}{\\ln\\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)}\n   \\]\n   We know that \\(\\frac{8}{7} = 1 + \\frac{1}{7}\\), so:\n   \\[\n   \\ln\\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right) = \\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{7}\\right)\n   \\]\n   Using the approximation \\(\\ln(1 + y) \\approx y\\) for small \\(y\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   \\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{7}\\right) \\approx \\frac{1}{7}\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   x \\approx 7 \\ln(7) \\cdot 7 = 49 \\ln(7)\n   \\]\n   However, we need to express \\(x\\) in the form \\(x = \\log_b 7^7\\). Notice that:\n   \\[\n   7^7 = \\left(7 \\cdot \\frac{8}{7}\\right)^7 = 8^7\n   \\]\n   So:\n   \\[\n   x = \\log_{8} 7^7 = \\log_b 7^7 = 7 \\log_b 8\n   \\]\n   From the previous steps, we know:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{7 \\ln(7)}{\\ln\\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)} \\approx 49 \\ln(7)\n   \\]\n   Therefore, we can equate:\n   \\[\n   7 \\log_b 8 = 49 \\ln(7)\n   \\]\n   Simplifying, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\log_b 8 = 7 \\ln(7)\n   \\]\n   Using the change of base formula, we have:\n   \\[\n   \\log_b 8 = \\frac{\\ln 8}{\\ln b}\n   \\]\n   So:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\ln 8}{\\ln b} = 7 \\ln(7)\n   \\]\n   Since \\(8 = 2^3\\), we have \\(\\ln 8 = \\ln(2^3) = 3 \\ln 2\\). Therefore:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3 \\ln 2}{\\ln b} = 7 \\ln(7)\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\(\\ln b\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   \\ln b = \\frac{3 \\ln 2}{7 \\ln(7)}\n   \\]\n   Using the property of logarithms \\(\\ln(a^b) = b \\ln(a)\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   \\ln b = \\ln(2^{3/7}) = \\frac{3}{7} \\ln 2\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   b = 2^{3/7}\n   \\]\n   So the value of \\(b\\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{2^{3/7}}\n   \\]",
