      "text": "To solve the equation \\(7^{x+7} = 8^x\\), we start by taking the natural logarithm of both sides to simplify the exponents:\n\n\\[\n\\ln(7^{x+7}) = \\ln(8^x)\n\\]\n\nUsing the logarithm power rule \\(\\ln(a^b) = b \\ln(a)\\), we can rewrite the equation as:\n\n\\[\n(x+7) \\ln(7) = x \\ln(8)\n\\]\n\nNext, we distribute the logarithms and then isolate \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\nx \\ln(7) + 7 \\ln(7) = x \\ln(8)\n\\]\n\nRearrange the terms to get all \\(x\\) terms on one side:\n\n\\[\nx \\ln(7) - x \\ln(8) = -7 \\ln(7)\n\\]\n\nFactor out \\(x\\) on the left side:\n\n\\[\nx (\\ln(7) - \\ln(8)) = -7 \\ln(7)\n\\]\n\nSolve for \\(x\\) by dividing both sides by \\(\\ln(7) - \\ln(8)\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-7 \\ln(7)}{\\ln(7) - \\ln(8)}\n\\]\n\nWe need to express \\(x\\) in the form \\(x = \\log_b 7^7\\). Using the change of base formula for logarithms, \\(\\log_b a = \\frac{\\log_c a}{\\log_c b}\\), we can rewrite \\(x\\) as:\n\n\\[\nx = \\log_b 7^7 = \\frac{\\log 7^7}{\\log b} = \\frac{7 \\log 7}{\\log b}\n\\]\n\nEquating this to our expression for \\(x\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{7 \\log 7}{\\log b} = \\frac{-7 \\ln(7)}{\\ln(7) - \\ln(8)}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\log 7 = \\ln(7)\\), we can substitute \\(\\ln(7)\\) for \\(\\log 7\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{7 \\ln(7)}{\\ln b} = \\frac{-7 \\ln(7)}{\\ln(7) - \\ln(8)}\n\\]\n\nWe can cancel \\(\\ln(7)\\) from both sides (assuming \\(\\ln(7) \\neq 0\\)):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{7}{\\ln b} = \\frac{-7}{\\ln(7) - \\ln(8)}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the right side of the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{7}{\\ln b} = \\frac{7}{\\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)}\n\\]\n\nSince the numerators are the same, we can equate the denominators:\n\n\\[\n\\ln b = \\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)\n\\]\n\nThus, \\(b = \\frac{7}{8}\\). The final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{7}{8}}\n\\]",
